                                        <view contentMode="scaleAspectFill" translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints="NO" id="KW8-zW-30C">
                                            <rect key="frame" x="0.0" y="676" width="375" height="734"/>
                                            <subviews>
                                                <label opaque="NO" userInteractionEnabled="NO" contentMode="left" horizontalHuggingPriority="251" verticalHuggingPriority="251" horizontalCompressionResistancePriority="751" verticalCompressionResistancePriority="751" lineBreakMode="tailTruncation" numberOfLines="0" baselineAdjustment="alignBaselines" adjustsFontSizeToFit="NO" translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints="NO" id="oVE-3E-Z0L">
                                                    <rect key="frame" x="20" y="0.0" width="335" height="734"/>
                                                    <string key="text">The coconut, often called the “tree of life,” is a remarkable fruit known for its versatility and nutritional value. Grown mainly in tropical regions, the coconut palm thrives in coastal areas and is a vital resource for millions of people worldwide.

Every part of the coconut is useful. The outer husk is used to make coir, which is turned into ropes, mats, and brushes. The hard shell serves as a container or can be carved into decorative items. Inside, the white flesh, or meat, is rich in healthy fats and can be eaten fresh, dried, or processed into coconut oil, cream, or milk. Coconut oil is especially prized for its antimicrobial properties and use in cooking, skincare, and hair care.

The clear liquid inside, known as coconut water, is a natural, refreshing drink packed with electrolytes, making it a popular hydration choice. It’s low in calories and fat, yet high in potassium, making it an ideal beverage for athletes and health-conscious individuals.

Culturally, the coconut holds significance in many traditions and religious ceremonies, symbolizing purity and prosperity. Economically, it supports many industries, including food, cosmetics, and handicrafts.

Coconuts are not only delicious and healthy but also environmentally sustainable. The tree requires minimal chemical inputs and contributes to soil and coastal protection.

In essence, the coconut is a gift from nature — nourishing, healing, and sustaining life in numerous ways. Whether in your kitchen, your skincare routine, or your celebrations, the coconut plays a valuable role in daily life.</string>
                                                    <fontDescription key="fontDescription" type="system" pointSize="15"/>
                                                    <color key="textColor" name="#092140"/>
                                                    <nil key="highlightedColor"/>
                                                </label>
